A||N||42||b lo 0||PLEESE help guys (& gals)!  Emotional crisis here!||||1||
Z||000000||b lo 0||08-15-2001||12:36 AM||||I'm still a little stupefied-my fiancee broke up with me tonight.  I am still very much in love with her.  She cites the fact that "I dont commit my life to God" as the reason.  She feels that even though I have been supportive through her emotional downturn lately and I have been the best friend a girl could have, I would have to share her religious viewpoint if we were to get married.  I feel very cheated, as I poured my heart and soul into our relationship over the past year, only to discover she wanted me to be something I'm not and will probably never be.  Don't get me wrong, I wouldn't trade a single day I spent with Kelly for the whole world, I just feel we both would have been saved a lot of tears had she told me this a year ago.  I'm so confused.  Please help, guys!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Fiero*101||08-15-2001||12:43 AM||||Ouch!!<P>are you Athesest?<P>Do you not live like she wants you too?<P>and your right she could of told you ALOT sooner!<P>Fiero*101||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula88||08-15-2001||12:47 AM||||What, are you like Pagan or something?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>That sounds like an excuse to me.  Otherwise, it would have been an issue all along and not just brought up recently.  IMHO.||||reg||12
Z||000003||b lo 0||08-15-2001||12:53 AM||||Actually, I am baptist, I just don't base my entire life around my religion.  On the other hand, her family is VERY religious.  I don't like Baptist churches because they are just fashion shows.  It was a big deal to her, she was pretty upset, too.  Thanks for the support guys!||||reg||1
Z||000004||EdsB52||08-15-2001||01:30 AM||||Sorry for you man. To me, this is just a preliminary attempt to get you to convert and commit more time than you want to the church. I don't see this as a religious agenda on her part as much as I see it as control. She may be just checking the parameters to see how much persuasion she has over you. I see it as good news/bad news. The good news is that I bet she'll be back, even if you don't reinitiate contact. The bad news is that this will always be a problem between you two and if you have a child, she may leverage you then. People that use leverage to attain one goal often use leverage to attain most goals in their life. It's a learned skill; possibly from the time she was a toddler/child. Either way, this is an early warning sign; tread with caution.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Galen||08-15-2001||05:13 AM||||Another relationship murdered my religion.. mmm.. sounds familar<P>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="purple">Ray</FONT> <FONT COLOR="gray">-</FONT> <FONT COLOR="purple">"With one drop of blood he realized his own mortallity, it was then he saw the true face of God. For he is not someone watching down on us, but he is in us." - Galen 2001</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000006||hugh||08-15-2001||07:32 AM||||It could be for the best.In this world there are many women that are as compatable with you as Kelly.I have been married almost 40 years,and I know that there are women out there that if I had married one of them I would have been just as happy.My wife is my best friend.Over the years,as you grew up,how many best friends have you had?probably a few.The same applies to wives(only not so often).||||reg||1
Z||000007||Tonker||08-15-2001||07:43 AM||||As long as you didn't bash her religion, I don't understand why she would have a problem. Did you tell her that you respect her beliefs and understand that religion is important to her, but that she should try to do the same for you?<P>It's funny to me that people who hold religion closely usually want people to respect their beliefs but often don't respect others' choice to not follow a particular faith. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_watchout.gif"><P>I'm sorry to hear of your bad news b lo, but I'm sure things will work out for you one way or another. Just remember the first few weeks are going to be pretty tough, try to hang out with your friends as much as possible, either here or in the "real world".||||reg||1
Z||000008||84Bill||08-15-2001||07:43 AM||||Ouch! They say love concurs all but I think in your case religion concurs love. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||California Kid||08-15-2001||08:16 AM||||Sorry to hear of the "break up", but it's a good thing it happened now instead of later.<P>Don't dwell on it, find a new chick to have fun with.  Time is very important, live your life to get the most out of every minute.  <P>Keep the fond memories of her for your private thoughts and disgard the rest. That special person will come into your life when you least suspect it.||||reg||1
Z||000010||DRH||08-15-2001||09:21 AM||||Hmmm... You are the same religion but you're not religious enough? Kinda sounds like someone else convinced her this was a problem??? Pastor, parent, friend???<P>Best of luck to you.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||ChadMan||08-15-2001||09:21 AM||||Man, I'm sorry to hear it, b lo.  I think for a happy marriage, you've got to see eye-to-eye on the Big Four:<P>* Money<BR>* Sex<BR>* Religion<BR>* Family<P>Any two people with serious differences on any of these issues need to reach a compromise, or they shouldn't get married.  <P>Unfortunately, there's probably no compromise to be made.  If she's a foot-of-the-cross Baptist, she probably believes you are hellbound unless you are born again as well.<P>I hate to say this, but you are probably going to have to either adopt her religion or let her go.  Any other compromise you hammer out won't last, because she'll still believe in her heart that you're on the Highway to Hell.<P>Edited to add:  None of this is meant to disparage anybody's religion.<p>[This message has been edited by ChadMan (edited 08-15-2001).]||||reg||9
Z||000012||lou_dias||08-15-2001||09:23 AM||||Ah religion...the age old divider of people and cause of most wars and deaths outside natural causes.<P>Yep it's still ahead of drugs..<P>I think this country should ban religion instead of drugs.  Hell they're both tax free anyway.<P>Oh I forgot... they won't legalize drugs so that politicians can declare a war on them to gain votes...<P>Notice how religion is kept out of campaigns...  Wouldn't want the Jew, Baptists, Born-Again, Prodestants and Catholics ...etc... to vote for the other guy.<P>Religion has obviously blinded/brainwashed this chick.  Leave her alone and maybe she'll come to her senses.||||reg||1
Z||000013||fierohoho||08-15-2001||10:20 AM||||You said she recently had an "emotional downturn". <P>I wonder if her family doesn't like you or your lack of religious fervor and used this vulnerable time in her life to beat her down emotionally into dumping you.<P> That may have been her way of getting them to leave her alone.<P>Maybe if she gets a little time alone she may wise up and call you.<P>I know it hurts man, took me a year to get over the first girl I fell in love with, hang in there and know we're hear to help. <P>Good luck. <P>------------------<BR>'84 SE 2.5l<BR>'85 Coupe 2.5l now getting a 3800<BR>'86 SE 2.5l||||reg||1
Z||000014||Raydar||08-15-2001||10:39 AM||||I know it hurts, but it's probably for the best. You guys need to agree on this, one way or the other, or not get married. <P>If she's had an "emotional downturn" (whatever that is), she may still be recovering. <BR>Not to say that she'll reconsider, or even that she should.<BR>Just an observation.<P>In any event, don't allow your hand to be forced. Conversely, don't try to force hers. <BR>Time heals.<P>Take care.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Ken Wittlief||08-15-2001||10:44 AM||||I went out with a girl for a few months when I was 19 - didnt develope into anything serious - she told me her parents said we could not have a long term relationship <P>because they were catholic and I wasnt!<P> Kinda blew me away - my first thought was, gee wiz, arnt you even gonna TRY to convert me?! (what? am I hopeless or something?!)<P> from your GF perspective a persons relationship with God IS more important that their realationship with their spouse - if she has completley put her life in Gods hands, and you havent, then she is right - there would be nothing but misery for both of you if you got married.<P> but this also has a ring of outside interference to it - maybe some pastor or parent or other influential person is putting her on the spot - testing her faith, or manipulating her life (ie: whats more important lady? your BF or God? "God!" ok prove it - dump the BF!)<P>  also this emotional downturn you mentioned - sounds like something major has happened in her life - this could be a knee jerk reaction to that, or it could be a carefully thought out response, a new dierection in her life.<P>  The 3 most important things for the 2 of you now is communication, communication, communication.  If she wants to break off the engagement then the two of you need to sit down several times and make SURE you understand each other, have a few days apart, talk again, think things over some more...<P> if she says her mind is already made up then insist - tell her she owes you this much - point out that you are both adults and if its true that you are not compatible on important issues then you will walk away from the realtionship as friends - but this is important to you and it needs to be resolved in a way you can BOTH feel good about in the end.<P> Also make it clear that if the religion thing is just an excuse to dump you, that you want to know the truth - you dont want your life to be based on lies - no matter how painfull it might be you want her to give you the straight dope. <P>   do this alone or with her minister, a consouler, a third person - you have both put a lot of time and energy into this relationship - make CERTAIN it doesnt end on a whim, or because of a misunderstanding, or because of a temporary thing she is going through.<P>  Best of luck.||||reg||10
Z||000016||Songman||08-15-2001||10:46 AM||||Sometimes two people of different religious backgrounds can make it, sometimes they can't. Depends on how 'fanatical' the involved parties are. I grew up Baptist. I have dated several Catholic women and although I didn't understand their religion, it never caused a problem. The girl I am dating now grew up Seventh Day Adventist. Not a problem even though I don't understand why they don't want to eat meat.<P>A more drastic version of this was my grandparents. My grandfather, a country singer like me, and my grandmother, a 40 year Baptist until she decided she was Church of Christ and thought music was a sin. You can imagine the fights that arose.<P>Personally, I think that anyone who would not be with you because of differences in religious beliefs is a little fanatical. No offense to your fiancee, but you did ask. Unless you are some devil worshipper and bite the heads off of small animals or something. My belief is that most religions are fairly close to the same thing, just minor differences to set them apart.<P>Let me quote a Merle Haggard song.. Surprise there huh?!? hehe<P><I>You never liked the clothes I wear on Sunday<BR>Just because I don't believe the way you do<BR>But I believe the Lord knows I'm unhappy<BR>Cause I can't be myself when I'm with you</I><P>Bottom line.. Don't change for anyone. Be true to yourself. Someone who loves you will accept that. <P>Sorry that you are having to go through this...<P>Oh! Let me tell you a classic line from my grandmother when I told her that one of my girlfriends was Catholic. She never even looked up at me... She said "Well, if that's how she wants to be." haha I thought I would fall down laughing! Anyone who looks down on someone because of religion needs to take another look at what their religion teaches.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif">  <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A><p>[This message has been edited by Songman (edited 08-15-2001).]||||reg||9
Z||000017||fierobaby||08-15-2001||11:01 AM||||wow that sucks! but of coourse religion just happens to be the "excuse". I don't know to much about the baptist faith. But I do know that religion is a big problem in relationships. I believe in God but do not agree with orginized religion. while my b/f is a baptist. anytime we talk about it we argue. So we don't talk about it. but someday we will have to and I hope its before we decided to be married and have kids. You are in a way lucky that she brought it up now. but also you need to talk with her about it. maybe you can work something out. explain to her exactly how you feel. Its worth a shot. well good luck sweetheart<P>love,<BR>Corinne<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||Jaygee79||08-15-2001||11:11 AM||||Yeah, what Corinne said   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I could see if it were a problem from the beginning, but why wait so long to say anything?  It sounds to me like someone said something to her.  Maybe her family convinced her of it.  I think you should try to talk to her.  Let her know how you feel.  If she expects you to respect her decision to be so devout, then she should have to respect your decision to not be that way, even if she doesn't agree with it.<p>[This message has been edited by Jaygee79 (edited 08-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||JSocha||08-15-2001||11:13 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"> Dang extremist, bible thumpers.  <P>Always letting small petty things get in the way and believing in some "Fire and Brimstone" philosophy.  Just because of some underlying thing they believe exsists someplace within their concisneous that it won't work out because someone above is going to disagree that both do not have a strong belief in a mighterier power and both will go to H-E-Double toothpick for the decision they make if they ain't in the same boat.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_bs.gif"><P>My biggest pet peeve, someone trying to force their religious beliefs on me, even though I don't disagree in theirs.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><P>BTW, just thinking after that frustrating comment, has anybody heard from Broken Hearted lately?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||8
Z||000020||TRiAD||08-15-2001||12:10 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by EdsB52:<BR><B>...To me, this is just a preliminary attempt to get you to convert and commit more time than you want to the church. I don't see this as a religious agenda on her part as much as I see it as control...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Not picking on you, MANY people here expressed the same idea...you just worded it better...<BR>----------<BR>While I'll be the first to agree with the "control" statements...women in general ARE control freaks...<P>This particular issue is MUCH bigger than you guys give it credit for...<BR>Here's her side (and I agree...)<P>She has made a 100% commitment to God, like it or not, agree with it or not. She has decided that He will be THE determining factor in her life...<I>all of it</I>. <P>This is a VERY big move for anyone to take, and her decision to break it off with you was I'm sure as devistating for her as it was for you. <P>You don't need to answer this part, it's real personal, just think about it. <P>More than likely in today's society, being engaged you guys were "doing the horizontal mambo" as it were. She has made a decision in her life that includes NOT having extra-marital sex. If she tried to get away from the sex, but keep the relationship otherwise intact and failed, this would be a major reason to break it off. If she didn't try this, than she didn't see it being successful, and never tried. <P>This isn't about her having "control", or trying to convert anyone...this is her taking "stock" of her life, and only including things that agree with her new found direction. If "religion" was getting in the way of you taking God seriously, then she has to decide to either help you through it (and quickly, since you were engaged), or "pruning the tree". She ended with the latter. <P>All in all, this is for the best. If you didn't agree on this major point, there was no basis for a continued relationship. She made this point paramount in her life, and it is now the most important aspect of any relationship she will go into. <P>Anyway, hope this helps...it's not a scam, and it's nothing trivial...this is THE only part of a relationship that matters to her anymore. <P>Keep on trucking...And good luck. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000021||frontal lobe||08-15-2001||12:25 PM||||I wish I COULD help you, but probably can't much other than to say how sorry I am for you.<P>If your relationship got to the fiancee level, then she was VERY dishonest and unfair to let it get that far without making her expectations very clear.  I don't fault her for HOW she chooses to live her religious life, but she should have let you know that up front.<P>ChadMan was very right.  Here is a perspective from someone with 13 successful years of marriage:<P>What do you want out of it?  A great marriage, a mediocre marriage, or a poor marriage?<P>If you want a GREAT one, then in the important areas of life (and you two would have to decide what areas are important-religion was in her particular case), are you a HELP and encouragement to each other?  Do you make it easier for each other to succeed in those areas?<P>If you want so-so or mediocre, then you don't hinder the other person but you really aren't much of a help.<P>If you want a poor one, you actually fight about important areas and hinder each other in those areas.<P>In her particular case, the level she is requesting isn't even the mediocre or so-so level, so it's not good enough for her that you leave her alone about it and let her do what she wants.  She apparently is looking for you to be an active help and encouragement in that area, and she is deciding that you aren't willing to do that.  Wow, a huge thing like that, she should have brought up and MADE CLEAR much earlier.  I'm sorry for you that she didn't.<P>I gave that long-winded explanation to point out why some people in certain areas can't just live-and-let-live within a marriage relationship when they can with other people.  The marriage relationship is a WAY DIFFERENT standard than other relationships you may have in life.||||reg||1
Z||000022||PAUL88GT||08-15-2001||01:53 PM||||If you still want to try to make it work, set up a meeting with her and her pastor.  Then, you may have more insight on some of her statements and ideas. It will help you make your decision on whether or not you should continue the relationship. I hope you get to feeling better.||||reg||1
Z||000023||Pilrulz||08-15-2001||02:20 PM||||wow, people on a car forum actually getting into a sort of religious discussion.  this is actually kind of cool.  you never get this kind of thing in an import forum.  actually, i am baptist, and my father is a pastor, but even so my entire life my entire life does not revolve around my religion.  still if you really love this girl you can try to work it out, but it could be that this was not meant to be and you just have to move on with your life.<P>hope everything goes well<P><BR>                             pil||||reg||12
Z||000024||b lo 0||08-15-2001||04:59 PM||||Hey guys.  I really appreciate the outpouring of support.  I do agree, I think her family had a LOT to do with this.  I was talking to her brother-in-law today (he's my boss) and he said when he married her sister, it was like her parents were bent on the fact that he had to be devoted to God first and foremost.  I think I'm just gonna try to move on.  Thing is, she loved cars, as I do, liked all the same stuff, just that one difference.  I guess one is enough when it's that big.  Say, any Fiero ladies want to hang out this weekend?  Nuthin serious, I'm on the rebound  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000025||starfighter007@msn.com||08-15-2001||06:54 PM||||Gosh I Ithoughtyou were nver gonna get over her. after about ten hours or so I thought we were gonna have to get you some counseling <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">But yeah you got the right Idea move on Hey maybe she'll change her mind when she sees that you can get along without god and her <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_damon.gif"> remember this. A good solid relationship between Two people is not like most people think 50/50,its 100%and 100% I wish you well||||reg||1
Z||000026||FieroKyle||08-16-2001||11:09 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Galen:<BR><B>Another relationship murdered my religion.. mmm.. sounds familar<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I am not relious at all myself, but its seems religion kills more than it helps. I like distanced myself with my friends a little when they tried to "conbert" me. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000027||pHoOl||08-17-2001||12:27 AM||||*strapping on fire proof clothes*  <BR>Okay, i'm gonna get nailed for this one, but religion is a very important thing to people.  Here is the thing.... if you know that she is super religious and you know that you are not, how is it that you couldn't have seen an issue coming up?  I realize that (assuming you were the one who proposed) when she accepted your proposal that she should have said something, but still.  <P>Just my two cents *putting on more aesbestos suits*<P>------------------<BR>Rice mobiles are like tampons... Every p@#%y has one.<P>AIM: phool88fiero<P>       <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/phoolsig1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000028||b lo 0||08-17-2001||12:58 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by pHoOl:<BR><B>*putting on more aesbestos suits*<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Asbestos?  That's gonna itch.  Don't you mean Nomex?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000029||LZeitgeist||08-17-2001||01:55 PM||||My first impression, not knowing either of you, was that it was an excuse she used to get out of the relationship. <P>CAUTION - VAST GENERALIZATIONS BASED ON MY PERSONAL EXPERIENCES LISTED BELOW<P>READ NO FURTHER - YOU HAVE BEEN WARNED<P><BR>Whenever women break up with a guy (in my experience), they will go to *EXTREME* lengths to prove to themselves that it wasn't *their* fault. It's *always* the guy's fault. Even if, to a normal, sane outside observer, she was completely off her frickin' rocker..<P>If you two are still able to communicate (I mean coherent, rational conversation), then there might be hope to find out what her *real* reason is for using an excuse to get out of the relationship. However, if she's not willing to communicate and isn't open to compromise, then even as hard as it is, MOVE ON. It's her loss. She probably had her mind made up a couple months ago, and was waiting for the right time to blame the failure of the situation on you.<P>Even if she feels in her heart that she *was* telling you the real reasons, if she was really interested in making things work with you, she would have A) stuck up for you with her family, and B) tried to work out a compromise with you, discussing the differences between your beliefs and hers and letting you know that the differences were causing her heartache. Since it sounds from your post that she did *neither* A nor B, I feel that her mind had been made up to break things off (for whatever reasons) for at least a month, and she was just waiting for circumstances to be right in order to make you feel like the one at fault. (Trust me, nearly every time I've been broken up with, they've made up their mind and waited a few weeks before they told me, instead of deciding and leaving, because if they just up and go, that makes it look like their fault, which is something they just won't allow to happen.)<P>Harshly spoken, yes, but all true and accurate in my experience. I'm not looking to start a 'guys vs gals' flamewar - I'm just stating the facts of the various situations that have happened to me in the past, in the hopes that you might find some possible explanation to help you feel better about your present situation. <P>I hate that it happened to you, man... heartbreak truly sucks. ||||reg||1
Z||000030||DaRkLoRD||08-17-2001||04:56 PM||||I met a girl this past winter, absolutely perfect... I could even get past her excessive drinking (ok, maybe not by many other people's standards, but I'm a bit conservative when it comes to drugs, alcohol etc) and smoking.. but she was very religious. I'm not..<P>I do consider myself a spiritual person.. I don't go to church, I don't read the christian bible.. (I have read a bit of the old testament, out of interest..) if I had to describe by beliefs, I'd say I'm off on some tangent that originated somewhere midway between pagan and buddhist..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>anyway, back to the story.. one night she asked me if I was religious, and I replied that I was not, though I said I considered myself spiritual. she said there's no difference.. I'm still kicking myself occasionally cause I stopped talking to her a while later (not totally for this reason..) but the fact that she was a bit of a hardcore catholic was a turn-off for me.. especially since she tried to take her ex bf to bible study occasionally..<P>I believe that spirituality without religion is possible... I believe that spirituality with religion is possible, and quite good because it gives guidance, but also meaning... but by far the worst situation, is religion without spirituality. If a person's religion becomes nothing more than empty words and rules, and going to church is something you do out of obligation to family members, or your own FEAR that if you don't go, then you will burn for all eternity in a christian hell.. (or the underworld of any other of the religions that have one.. take your pick.) then it's likely not a positive experience.. anyone ever heard the term "god-fearing person"? if a religion teaches people to fear their god.. then wow, what a great religion.. where do I sign up?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>I'm not trying to offend anyone here, just trying (probably unsuccessfully) to make a point that religious differences can cause problems in relationships.. big problems..<P>anyway, take it easy Greg..<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000031||Ken Wittlief||08-17-2001||05:26 PM||||Wow this simple request for help sure has brought out a wide range of responses.<P>Ive always viewed religion as mans attempt to reach out to, interact, or appease god - with the goal of getting something or some favor from god.<P>Spirtuality: the understanding that there is more to us than flesh and bones, that a part of us exists independant from the physical world, and will continue to exist after we die.<P> so yes you can be religious without ever actually encountering god or any kind of supernatural experience - a way to explain and deal with stuff you dont understand - doing rituals with no feedback as to whether anyone is out there to respond to you - esp bad if you then take whatever happens and interprete it as gods response, or gods will or direction.<P>and you can be spiritual and acknowledge your soul/spirit, but still never encounter god - alot of the major religions are like this - they think we are god - we just dont realize it yet - and when we meditate or fast or evolve enough we will wake up some day and achieve cosmic conscienceness (suddenly realize that we have been god all along).<P>I dont know if we even have a term for having a personal relationship with God Himself, on His level, on His terms, with the clear distinction that God is the creator of the universe, and that we are NOT Him.<P> so from some peoples perspective, yes you can be religious in a sense, and tolerate and even marry someone of a different religion, if your religion doesnt really mean all that much to you.<P>  but if you got some lady who spends an hour every morning praying to God to let her know what she should do today, and tomorrow, what she should do with her time and her money, and you are not a part of that, then you can find yourself married to someone who is gonna come home from church some day and announce that God had directed her to go to Tibet and be a missionary...<P>  It sounds like your GF is close to being on this level with her church and her beliefs - if you are not then you are doing the right thing to accept her decision and cut the ties.||||reg||1
Z||000032||maryjane||08-18-2001||02:28 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by b lo 0:<BR><B> <BR>Asbestos?  That's gonna itch.  Don't you mean Nomex?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Don't know about the rest, but military nomex is useless. I've got skin graft scars to prove it. I'd rather itch than burn again anyday.<BR>B lo O: Feel for ya, nobody ever died from a broken heart, lots of us wanted to. That's another pain I never want again. If she's that important to you, talk it out. You may never change her mind, or she change yours, but the two of you might come  to some understanding about it anyway.<BR>Don||||reg||1
Z||000033||b lo 0||08-19-2001||01:51 AM||||Thanks for all the support, guys.  Kelly and I were able to calm down a little tonight, and talked for an hour and a half on the phone (she moved into her dorm today).  We were able to see where we were both coming from, and we are in the process of working it out.  I have to admit, I am not as spiritual as I used to be.  We both understand each other better, and we have a date (not engaged, but not as just friends, either) for next Friday to feel things out.  We are meeting in the middle here, as I am trying to live my life more in God's eye, and she is trying to separate her religious and personal lives.  I am really glad we are working on this, and the support of everyone here on the Forum has meant so much to me through this hard time.  Steve, thanks for the kind words.  I miss talkin to ya, bud!  Thanks again for being friends!<P>*Group hug!*||||reg||1
